#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230035} :activity/id #uuid "10505396-7ff3-52ce-80bf-1cab768fe5a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230152 :tutorial/content "When solving ODEs numerically:\n\n- The solution is advanced in many steps.\n\n- Each step introduces a small error (LTE).\n\n- These errors propagate forward (contributing to GTE).\n\nStability describes how a numerical method responds to these small perturbations.\n\nA method is **stable** if small errors remain controlled as the computation proceeds.\nA method is **unstable** if small errors can grow rapidly, even when the true solution stays well-behaved.\n\nStability determines how local errors turn into global errors: a method may be accurate in a single step, but if it is unstable, the global error can grow without bound.\n\nOne way to study stability is to test a numerical method on the following **test equation**:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nThe exact solution of the test equation is $y(x) = e^{-\\lambda x},$ which satisfies $y(x) \\to 0$ as $x \\to \\infty.$\n\nWe say that a numerical method with update formula \n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y\n$$\n\n is **stable** for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation, the numerical solution also satisfies\n\n$$\ny_n \\to 0 \\:\\text{ as }\\: n \\to \\infty.\n$$\n\nTo see how this works in practice, let's analyze the stability of Euler's method." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac3e5ad1-de8e-5400-81a3-ea12a0eae4b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c441e4ca-9601-5bd8-98b8-304592a68f49" :step/math-academy-id 594522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230181 :tutorial/content "We will now check the stability of Euler's method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n, y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\lambda {y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)((1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's define $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x.$ Then, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$ The function $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is called the **stability function** of Euler's method.\n\nExpanding this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1 \\\\ -1<1-\\lambda Δx & <1 \\\\ -2<-\\lambda Δx & <0 \\\\ 0<\\lambda Δx & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of Euler's method for the cases $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ and $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ with $\\lambda > 0$ fixed.\n\n![](images/30/30dc27c192c43aa4fb57ed79f8412b85d106e662ce0416f814621d482d2466ea.png)\n\nNotice that, for $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2,$ the numerical values converge to $0$ (stable), whereas for $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ the values oscillate between $-1$ and $1$ and do not converge to $0$ (unstable).\n\nFor values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 2,$ the numerical solution would grow in magnitude at each step, leading to rapidly diverging values." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee73cf90-ef1e-5e56-b1f5-449c9ca0a73f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14463 :tutorial/title "Stability of Euler's Method"} :step/id #uuid "d9cda7a3-3be2-5ad6-8073-62f51df6f6d8" :step/math-academy-id 594523 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228903 :question/id #uuid "bf22c16e-fc49-5ba5-9c64-89ee66d0a6e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21636"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050155}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"} :step/id #uuid "34e7c45b-1aa6-5e2a-a0c0-4c70c1a2c90a" :step/math-academy-id 594524 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228905 :question/id #uuid "0575b9de-7811-547e-90f4-e7a431dac733" :question/math-academy-id "e-21638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443993}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"} :step/id #uuid "b09d6c35-55a3-5805-92dc-cd2b2766a54d" :step/math-academy-id 594527 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230182 :tutorial/content "In general, when a numerical method\n$y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$\nis applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nwe can write the update in the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the **stability function** of the method.\n\nFor stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a geometric sequence, this decays to $0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$\n\nThe set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the **stability region** of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called **absolutely stable**.\n\nWe've seen that Euler's method is *not* absolutely stable, since its stability region is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$ But what about the implicit Euler method? Let's find out." :tutorial/id #uuid "b296dcea-4d6f-59ab-8621-78aab4024f7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14464 :tutorial/title "Stability Functions, Stability Regions, and Absolute Stability"} :step/id #uuid "e903f273-6080-5032-b3e1-8452a8255818" :step/math-academy-id 594531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230185 :tutorial/content "Now, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\lambda {y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+\\lambda Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThe method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1.$\n\nSince $1 + \\lambda\\Delta x > 1$ for any positive step size $\\Delta x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n$$\n\nfor all $\\Delta x > 0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable!\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of both the explicit and the implicit Euler methods for the value $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.$ \nWe saw before that the explicit method oscillates between the values $-1$ and $1$ and does not converge to $0.$\n\nConversely, the numerical steps of the implicit Euler method converge to $0$ quite quickly for the same step size!\n\n![](images/08/083b5c0193a2a35648434cae4dcf1820fd47c86202c13a7e7e2e078d45d52bdb.png)\n\nImplicit methods tend to be more numerically stable than explicit methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "1648c2df-353a-5461-abab-2c546f249a47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14484 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "64f9d725-bde4-51b6-8bdd-15f6dfc6bbe2" :step/math-academy-id 594532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228904 :question/id #uuid "6d580b90-1fbf-541c-8e63-97bb40ce0f3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497342}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"} :step/id #uuid "f63a1021-37c7-556c-a328-99fccaab7a5a" :step/math-academy-id 594533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230183 :tutorial/content "A table of some numerical methods, their stability functions, and stability regions is shown below. These results assume the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ (where $\\lambda > 0$) with step size $\\Delta x$.\n\n| Numerical Method | Stability Function $R(\\lambda \\Delta x)$ | Stability Region $|R(\\lambda \\Delta x)| < 1$ | Absolutely Stable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Euler's | $1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$ | $0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Implicit Euler | $\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Trapezoidal | $\\dfrac{1 - \\lambda \\Delta x/2}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x/2}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Modified Euler | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Runge-Kutta (RK4) | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "58221058-7c32-5e60-97f1-2702853713f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14470 :tutorial/title "Table of Results"} :step/id #uuid "c40d6fad-d59f-58ff-81a3-649a536718a5" :step/math-academy-id 594537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228906 :question/id #uuid "e522ef7a-a93a-5711-9193-6afb8faa4327" :question/math-academy-id "e-21639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050891}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519590}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"} :step/id #uuid "c24c474e-a160-5185-a102-eee0357694fb" :step/math-academy-id 594538}] :activity/title "Stability of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229253}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497461} :activity/id #uuid "cbb9bff8-0fb7-50be-adc5-7623e34f2040" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e50e8ab-e411-586f-a3d4-dc2e4fcd7cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466065} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8afd2e-ae6c-570d-a8ff-76907c67d059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b9e8dd-90d0-51c7-be44-1b2eb0a32755" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56cefe7-8454-534e-b476-cc7a1d062420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfa897d-e2a7-5a0a-9895-d4e337ada051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1061fc5-5342-526c-88c1-d457329519a0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25c152c-cde8-5b9b-a39d-1c434bef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} {:answer/id #uuid "2db53487-34f5-58b2-a4f4-a19aaa44c64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c682ebed-3244-5b41-a799-d3bf75036c38" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3833cd4-b47d-541f-9a24-3a41b77197eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466071} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5fdda5-251a-5d00-8098-412f1be153f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072} :answer-field/id #uuid "030e73ec-670f-572f-9967-01c8e343d51e" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30787f50-63d0-5577-9588-239251d45d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333092" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=2.0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."} :step/id #uuid "61a72ca7-449a-52f3-bf8e-93853046baf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497468}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a567c91c-11d7-5940-8adc-f09cd2d0fddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462263} {:answer/id #uuid "31e3f346-f2a6-5edb-91d3-5031fb7f980a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462264} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fcd9ee7-351d-5544-b31e-d5339038bd16" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ffee5da-bd54-550d-bf31-632cd5a50672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462265} {:answer/id #uuid "76edda26-bda9-5fec-afe7-be2d775dfcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462266} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6255aa2-753f-5c9f-ae01-074e1e981e62" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556da2f8-4988-5c21-afbc-f09ab1f4e7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462267} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab302c1-916a-5999-ad86-77bdb3a80d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462268} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c582a6-0da6-593d-81ce-223c901e263a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c10f69f1-f4a6-5042-b2b9-344a17db3922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462269} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaa2383-6061-5021-8da6-142a8d04e77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462269} :answer-field/id #uuid "7225a2f4-1737-5b07-bd07-4b0019b5b353" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3afc7fc5-d735-57e5-883a-73d956edd87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332533" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.67$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.20$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=1.80$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=2.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.67$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.20$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.80$ falls *within* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.25$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for the values $\\lambda Δx=0.67,$ $\\lambda Δx=1.20,$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.80.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "a3d07d02-d1d6-58a1-be52-3ef85dfeafe1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497467}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c495fcbe-46e4-5570-a5d1-28127f914a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228101} {:answer/id #uuid "db63787a-f64d-520f-b951-801e1f9c89e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228102} {:answer/id #uuid "f9029063-2276-5c5e-aaae-5419b3c1391e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228103} {:answer/id #uuid "62130c11-c8cc-5bfd-aa1b-21009a57c496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228104} {:answer/id #uuid "75075a4d-6bc9-5c33-a9f0-607303832709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228104} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b55e392-d0cf-5935-be82-e8780c5e7cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e90b20b3-e8ec-5eff-9813-0425b7acc64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332744" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|1-0.4+\\frac{(0.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{419}{625}| \\\\  & =0.670\\,4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|1-1+\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3}{8}| \\\\  & =0.375<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2)| & =|1-2+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =0.333…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3+\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11}{8}| \\\\  & =1.375<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} :step/id #uuid "ac815580-094f-5483-a534-42ce5406ed84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c86d4ff-e60d-5ed0-9b7e-51fd840bb413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496789} {:answer/id #uuid "469a895b-ff19-5c22-9203-6eb75d050f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496812} {:answer/id #uuid "501f49f4-5d2c-516d-b744-0038bbab282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496833} {:answer/id #uuid "525fd160-0089-59c0-a76b-1ab57865318a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496836} {:answer/id #uuid "80c052ba-48ac-56d0-8ff1-de202eea8817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496923} :answer-field/id #uuid "a124cc3f-17e0-53b5-aed5-2049adc80c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25d64691-5b6a-542f-814d-ce1c552ea7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+4{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+4Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+4Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.1.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "ad7e16c0-6c4a-571d-8f1d-bcb5e05cd25b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4e0507-df7f-5aa5-98f1-856c82ebe79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496756} {:answer/id #uuid "748cd873-307f-5e75-9f8f-77a3d766273d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496905} {:answer/id #uuid "78dba489-7ba7-5d7e-a78f-ab1fdacb56a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496913} {:answer/id #uuid "d6203cbd-f862-5bd5-a4a5-da7556b09cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497105} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d3c51b-b3a8-5879-b596-2a32d0b50eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497105} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48506ec-17d9-5b93-9a61-0014927029a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74bb58bc-5799-594f-9fe1-c2f6de777ad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334247" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.3$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=4$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=0.05$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.3}| \\\\  & =0.4347…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{5}| \\\\  & =0.2<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.05,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.05)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.05}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.05}| \\\\  & =0.9523<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"} :step/id #uuid "e7a32cad-b05c-53f3-8a55-0a0ff5e0e6be"}] :activity/title "Stability of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496657}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519671} :activity/id #uuid "e7eca05f-e99e-590a-bf49-64802c41086f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6581fb-1cd9-5f47-8ff9-5979ac885085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} {:answer/id #uuid "523d5a9b-bdc7-5943-9f2e-bd9519abc022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b68c3fb-619e-551d-af87-e5d8bc615d9c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "883b44a2-bf01-5f77-a3ef-4667d3673879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450442} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d407e9-bdde-51d4-838c-250cb8be5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e6b0e4-794c-511c-a8b6-8176c4d57f20" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b751b7e-6ae0-51fb-a6ba-d823ccb81759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450444} {:answer/id #uuid "f0455e55-18d0-5a46-be37-2c94e8c15bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bd6593-a175-58b8-8df8-17cd965f7378" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d9c5801-9019-57ca-93c4-8d5e18dac042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} {:answer/id #uuid "03fa487c-d0a5-5d09-b69c-f51e9fd595b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e87aa0-6c37-57e0-9829-c738cc1d85a5" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55adb6e6-36a2-5272-9189-7161d38a92cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333578" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =7,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$\n- **II.** The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$\n- **III.** The method is absolutely stable.\n- **IV.** The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "18bfeff9-da8e-5adf-b0b0-f3f134960c7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a83fe5b-8074-5c2d-93ca-132b209dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519009} {:answer/id #uuid "675fbddc-fb55-57b4-97c1-275b6152cefd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519143} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cea126-5b1a-5675-bae4-21fe72227319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519342} {:answer/id #uuid "da511c1c-87de-5875-ad76-4fd6abdb409d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519351} {:answer/id #uuid "df235f97-7d7c-5f76-8fe1-ed5b46b28e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519143} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7ebb238-bf1c-5419-8e2b-8b67c38f85fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4d56835-15e1-5dd9-b189-b250c69cae71" :question/math-academy-id "q-334717" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.3$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.7$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.3)| & =|1-0.3+\\frac{(0.3)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.1)| & =|1-1.1+\\frac{(1.1)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{101}{200}| \\\\  & =0.505<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.7)| & =|1-1.7+\\frac{(1.7)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|1-2.9+\\frac{(2.9)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{461}{200}| \\\\  & =2.305>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} :step/id #uuid "236a5063-a320-5c79-a165-40f007e87d4e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4e6a2e4-a130-5f3a-b5af-59edc314f0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466125} {:answer/id #uuid "999a1a3e-f07f-51c1-89c7-2c2e5ce92eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466126} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ab3db8-e08d-5ad8-a06f-cc382521ee79" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b7b5bcd-cddb-55f0-b269-79a8ab77d160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466127} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaf88bc-383d-5594-a096-e0a34b4de52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466127} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7bad4e-048c-587a-a7df-e955747cbe46" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119754ec-a866-5ea0-9294-1804c408bd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466129} {:answer/id #uuid "97a0407f-8581-544f-91ac-3d5c1b386e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466130} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4ce22c-631f-5997-9059-e3bcdc6038d9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466908} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2866b660-be68-5c27-a884-63d993e90ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466131} {:answer/id #uuid "a67b55b5-3a28-54fb-995f-718d03c297f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466132} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb60801-a619-5246-8b60-f108f387db98" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c9db1fa-3df6-5592-9112-986f10641d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-334014" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$\n- **II.** $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda }$\n- **III.** $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }$\n- **IV.** $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.4.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <0.4<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."} :step/id #uuid "50ccc479-0bdf-5248-b544-a38b78cc03a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcfddc24-c58f-5c4f-84e9-ef551982185b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471427} {:answer/id #uuid "9769274f-fb36-5fbc-8541-fc90883166a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471428} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a7fbf7-0d41-5041-aae1-a39e147b182f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f185f3b-0a68-5abd-a8c2-38bf014f58b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471429} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8a98cb-e1a9-56a3-92c7-055782d9153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471430} :answer-field/id #uuid "74f644d8-2edd-5b0b-937d-f2bfddc4567b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088c5cde-2edc-582f-8a1e-928e3b8226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471431} {:answer/id #uuid "dfda1d96-e20f-55b1-8aa9-981ec2a0f203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471431} :answer-field/id #uuid "79254c88-eae6-5edf-bb54-1cc11619fc3f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472221} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753fff6f-e13f-5c5d-ae71-087ae88e049e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471433} {:answer/id #uuid "e59e1c38-8632-55cd-89ae-49999007c409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471434} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3380c0-50d4-5961-9f40-7aaca9597d4f" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ea2a49c-9462-54d3-b8e9-50d1002b7880" :question/math-academy-id "q-334775" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.65$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.35$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=3.6$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=1.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.65)| & =|1-0.65+\\frac{(0.65)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{449}{800}| \\\\  & =0.561\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.35,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.35)| & =|1-1.35+\\frac{(1.35)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{449}{800}| \\\\  & =0.561\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.6)| & =|1-3.6+\\frac{(3.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{97}{25}| \\\\  & =3.88>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.25)| & =|1-1.25+\\frac{(1.25)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{32}| \\\\  & =0.531\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} :step/id #uuid "f438fdd3-89dd-5c2a-9c44-3d2afb1adf2d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554a3f25-c6d4-56f1-97a1-e72f6e996868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450424} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf7d503-a537-5980-a459-e3d845c2a7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425} :answer-field/id #uuid "2668b372-5058-5bcf-87d1-b3dc18aa9e3e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1d305cf-a373-552c-84d7-e16b441a5d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450426} {:answer/id #uuid "20b79c3e-cfe8-5182-8d84-c0226c536bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9d7e34-4fe0-5a4b-be27-0a630b6ca736" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a745d4d-5653-50dd-8c45-7098cb48f439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450428} {:answer/id #uuid "05afd7a7-372e-5f9d-8fe6-0eaf11538d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6caa9e2-7fc4-57e0-84db-0278682927f4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451168} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75636e46-7f14-5059-a314-ce331ac05304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} {:answer/id #uuid "2a562ffb-8f60-5832-8dc2-4814364ea999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} :answer-field/id #uuid "e64ab8c0-b38a-58c0-8fc6-2259dd4a7a0d" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a2ae136-3885-5a0c-a306-c58f92aeffc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333572" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$\n- **II.** The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$\n- **III.** The method is absolutely stable.\n- **IV.** The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "fd7e31ca-0961-5f9c-aed7-3bd1d98457f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519614}}] :activity/title "Stability of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518989}]}
